---
// Terms page. Written 2026-08-24 on Hidde's instruction ("ga maar voorwaarden
// maken en toevoegen aan het inlogproces"), because the app is about to start
// keeping what readers send instead of leaving it on their phone, and the
// moment the first photograph lands there is an obligation attached to it.
//
// The structure is the ordinary consumer-app one and nothing here is invented:
// what the service is, accounts, the licence you grant on what you send, what
// we do with it, deletion, and the limits of what we promise. AllTrails' own
// terms could not be read for reference: their robots.txt disallows ClaudeBot
// from everything but a handful of marketing pages, so the convention copied
// here is the shared one rather than theirs specifically.
//
// NOT reviewed by a lawyer. Hidde knows; it is his name on it.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BASE_URL } from "../lib/schema";

const canonical = `${BASE_URL}/terms`;
---
<Base title="Terms" description="The terms for using Ancient Trees and for what you send us." canonical={canonical}>
<main class="content-page">
  <h1>Terms</h1>
  <div class="prose-block">
    <p>Ancient Trees maps remarkable old trees and the walks that string them together. These terms cover using the site and the app, and what happens to anything you send us. They are written to be read, not to be survived.</p>

    <h2>Using it</h2>
    <p>The map, the trees and their stories are free to read, with or without an account. Do not scrape the site wholesale, resell the data as your own, or use it to harm a tree or the people who look after one.</p>

    <h2>Your account</h2>
    <p>An account is one email address and the trees you keep. No password. In the app you may also choose a display name and a picture so other people can find you and follow you; both are optional and neither has to be your real name or face. You can delete the account at any time from your account page, and deleting it removes your address and everything attached to it.</p>

    <h2>Other people</h2>
    <p>A name and a picture other people can see come with one rule, and it is not negotiable: <strong>nothing offensive, abusive, threatening, obscene or impersonating somebody else.</strong> There is no tolerance for it here and no warning system to work through. If you see any of it, the three dots beside a person let you report them or block them; a report reaches us and a block hides them from you straight away. We read every report, we remove what does not belong, and we close the accounts of people who put it there.</p>
    <p>Do not use somebody else's name, face or photographs as your own, and do not use the app to harass anybody.</p>

    <h2>What you send us</h2>
    <p>When you photograph a tree, add one we do not have, correct something, or send a tip, that reaches us. By sending it you give us permission to store it, to show it on the site and in the app, and to use it in material about the platform itself. That permission is worldwide and free of charge.</p>
    <p><strong>Once a photograph of yours is published on a tree's page, that permission stays.</strong> It survives you deleting your account, and it is the one thing that does. The reason is the map itself: a tree page that loses its picture is a worse page for everybody who comes after, and the picture is often the only one that exists of that tree. What does not stay is you. There is no name beside it to begin with, the link to your account goes, and what is left is a photograph of a tree that nothing ties to you. If you want the photograph itself gone as well, ask, and we take it off.</p>
    <p>You keep ownership of your photographs. Nothing here transfers that, we do not become the owner by publishing one, and nothing stops you using your own picture anywhere else, including selling it.</p>
    <p>Send only what is yours to send. A photograph you took yourself is fine; one you found somewhere is not.</p>

    <h2>What we do with it</h2>
    <p>We decide what goes on the public map. Most of what arrives is checked, and some of it is published; the rest stays yours and stays in your own collection whether or not it ever joins the map everybody sees.</p>
    <p><strong>A published photograph carries no name at all.</strong> If a tree has no picture, or yours is better than the one there, we may put it on that page, and nothing beside it says who took it. The app says so on the screen where you take it. We do not publish your name or your address, and we do not publish your collection: what you have ticked off is yours and is shown to nobody.</p>
    <p>A photograph often carries where it was taken and when. We use that to place the tree. We do not build a picture of where you go, we do not sell it, and we do not share it with advertisers.</p>

    <h2>Removing things</h2>
    <p>Ask and it goes. Delete your account and everything attached to it goes with it: your address, your collection, your sightings and their photographs, your name and your picture.</p>
    <p><strong>One thing stays, and it is the exception worth reading.</strong> A photograph of yours that we already published on a tree's page stays on that page. Your name comes off it, and nothing is left connecting it to you. A tree you added that we published stays on the map for the same reason, as a tree rather than as something of yours. Both are unlinked within a day rather than instantly, because these pages are built rather than served live. If you want the photograph gone too, or want one removed without deleting your account, <a href="/contribute?kind=privacy">send a privacy request</a> naming it, and it goes.</p>

    <h2>What we do not promise</h2>
    <p>Trees fall, gates close, councils fell things without telling us, and a pin can be wrong. We say so on the page when we know a location is rough, and we correct what readers report, but we cannot promise that any tree is still standing or that you can reach it today.</p>
    <p>Walking is yours to judge. Routes here are suggestions worked out from where the trees stand, not signposted trails, and they are not checked for traffic, surface or safety. Look where you are going.</p>

    <h2>Changes, and the law</h2>
    <p>When these terms change, the date below changes with them. Dutch law applies.</p>
    <p>Questions, or something you want removed: <a href="/contribute?kind=privacy">write to us</a>.</p>
    <p><em>Last updated: 3 September 2026</em></p>
  </div>
</main>
</Base>
